{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.Sigma235FalseThetaExponent","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.Sigma235FalseThetaExponent","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.sigma235_lz_exponent_not_integral","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.sigma235_lz_exponent_not_integral","kind":"theorem","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Sigma235FalseThetaExponent.lean#L87-L91","doc":"The `n²/120` normalisation is **not** integral: at `n = 1` the exponent numerator `n²`\nleaves remainder `1` on division by `120`. Stated as the sharp negative so the two\nnormalisations cannot be silently identified. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.sigma235_exponent_low","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.sigma235_exponent_low","kind":"theorem","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Sigma235FalseThetaExponent.lean#L79-L85","doc":"The four exponents Jacoby–Lawrence's series actually carries at the four positive\nsupport residues: `0, 1, 3, 7`. These are the numbers a reader can check against p. 55,\nand they are integers exactly because of `sigma235_sq_eq_120_mul_add_one`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.sigma235_dvd_sq_sub_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.sigma235_dvd_sq_sub_one","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Sigma235FalseThetaExponent.lean#L73-L77","doc":"Divisibility form of `sigma235_sq_eq_120_mul_add_one`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.sigma235_sq_eq_120_mul_add_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.sigma235_sq_eq_120_mul_add_one","kind":"theorem","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Sigma235FalseThetaExponent.lean#L54-L71","doc":"**The exponent is integral.** On the support of `χ`, `n²` is `1` more than a multiple\nof `120`; equivalently `(n²−1)/120 ∈ ℤ`.\n\nThis is the fact that makes the Jacoby–Lawrence normalisation `q^{(n²−1)/120}` a genuine\npower series, and pins the discrepancy with `q^{n²/120}` to the single constant `q^{1/120}`\nrather than to any `n`-dependent difference. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.Sigma235Support","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.Sigma235Support","kind":"def","line":48,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Sigma235FalseThetaExponent.lean#L48-L52","doc":"The eight residues mod 60 on which the `Σ(2,3,5)` character `χ` is nonzero:\n`{1, 11, 19, 29}` where `χ = +1`, and their partners `60 − r` where `χ = −1`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}